From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "A.Cervantez" <155030226+Ywach404@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 12:38:01 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] runtime: make UIKit's SDL calls on the main thread on
 visionOS

The first simulator run stopped in SDL_VideoInit on the runtime's
thread: UIKit_SuspendScreenSaver set the idle timer, FrontBoard asserted
it was off the main thread, and the process took a SIGTRAP. Patch 0010
had made the runtime's thread SDL's main thread, but on visionOS UIKit
only accepts the process main thread, which the host app's SwiftUI run
loop owns.

So the main thread becomes SDL's main thread. rt_host_start, which the
host calls there, declares it ready before the runtime's thread exists,
and every SDL call that reaches UIKit is made there through rt_main_hop
(runtime/main_hop.h), a dispatch_sync to the main queue that runs in
place when already on it, so hops nest. SDL_RunOnMainThread does not
fit: SDL drains its queue only from an SDL_PumpEvents on its main
thread, which nothing on the SwiftUI side calls. The wait is safe
because the main thread never waits for the runtime: the host's done
callback is asynchronous, nothing calls SDL_Quit, and the runtime ends
with _exit.

The hops are coarse. Video init is one (with SDL's iOS event pump off,
since SwiftUI already runs the main run loop and delivers UIKit's events
to SDL's views). Creating, showing, resizing and freeing a window is one
each, which carries the configuration, placement, text input, input
window and gamepad start along with it. The whole pump is one, events,
input script, cursor, warp, relative mode and the full screen tick
included. The bridge's native window resize is one. Waiting for events
watches SDL's queue without pumping, since SDL_WaitEventTimeout would
pump on the calling thread.

The window's pixel size, which SDL reads from the UIView and the present
path asks for every frame, is cached at the end of every hop and read
from the cache off the main thread. The bridge's D3D9 thread guard asks
for the guest's primary thread instead of SDL's main thread, which is
the same thread on the Mac and no longer is here.

Audio, timers, hints, event pushes, gamepad state and the window and
display fields SDL keeps in its own structs stay where they are called.
The Mac build is unchanged.
